<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    The standing roles the two shipped stations carry (system: offices, src/SimWorld.Core/Offices). These are
    ordinary Work.RoleDefs and nothing about the class is special — what makes them different is that an
    OfficeDef names one, and OfficeManager therefore treats "carries this role" as "holds that seat". They are
    reserved for their office in the same sense a defName is reserved: assigning one by hand, or sweeping a
    whole settlement with WorkPolicyUtility.ApplyRoleToPopulation, is corrected on the next office sweep
    rather than forbidden.

    Its own file rather than an addition to Roles.xml: a new file cannot conflict with another lane's edit,
    and DefLoader.AddDirectory loads every XML under the folder recursively (CLAUDE.md).

    Both roles are built from the existing 20 core WorkTypeDefs (WorkTypeDefs/WorkTypes.xml) — a role is a
    lens on work that already exists, which is the rule Roles.xml set for itself and this keeps.
  -->

  <RoleDef>
    <defName>Steward</defName>
    <label>steward</label>
    <description>Speaks for the settlement: deals with the people who arrive at it, and sees its public works finished. Everything else this citizen is willing to do comes after.</description>
    <!--
      A settlement leader's two jobs, in the terms this port already has work types for: the people who turn
      up at the gate (Warden — recruiting, feeding and holding outsiders) and what the town is building
      (Construction). Deliberately not Research or Art, which are the eldership's; two stations that
      emphasized the same work would be one station with two names.
    -->
    <emphasizedWorkTypes>
      <li>Warden</li>
      <li>Construction</li>
    </emphasizedWorkTypes>
  </RoleDef>

  <RoleDef>
    <defName>Elder</defName>
    <label>elder</label>
    <description>Keeps what the civilization knows and how it tells its own story. Everything else this citizen is willing to do comes after.</description>
    <!--
      The eldership is memory: Research (what the civilization has worked out) and Art (how it says so).
      Research is a real mechanic here rather than flavour — WorkGiver_Research drives JobDriver_Research and
      moves the current project — so a civilization whose last founder has died genuinely loses someone
      putting research first.
    -->
    <emphasizedWorkTypes>
      <li>Research</li>
      <li>Art</li>
    </emphasizedWorkTypes>
  </RoleDef>

</Defs>
